Chapter: Mqtt
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Units (Duration)
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 MQTT v5.0 protocol types and packet builders for IoT messaging.
 Implements the wire protocol per OASIS MQTT v5.0 (March 2019).
 Each builder returns a List Integer (the raw packet bytes).
 Transport is handled by the caller (TCP, TLS, or WebSocket).

Section: Packet Types

  MqttPacketType =
    | MqttConnect
    | MqttConnack
    | MqttPublish
    | MqttPuback
    | MqttPubrec
    | MqttPubrel
    | MqttPubcomp
    | MqttSubscribe
    | MqttSuback
    | MqttUnsubscribe
    | MqttUnsuback
    | MqttPingreq
    | MqttPingresp
    | MqttDisconnect

  mqtt-packet-id : MqttPacketType -> Integer
  mqtt-packet-id (t) =
   when t
    is MqttConnect -> 1
    is MqttConnack -> 2
    is MqttPublish -> 3
    is MqttPuback -> 4
    is MqttPubrec -> 5
    is MqttPubrel -> 6
    is MqttPubcomp -> 7
    is MqttSubscribe -> 8
    is MqttSuback -> 9
    is MqttUnsubscribe -> 10
    is MqttUnsuback -> 11
    is MqttPingreq -> 12
    is MqttPingresp -> 13
    is MqttDisconnect -> 14

Section: QoS Levels

  MqttQos =
    | QosAtMostOnce
    | QosAtLeastOnce
    | QosExactlyOnce

  mqtt-qos-value : MqttQos -> Integer
  mqtt-qos-value (q) =
   when q
    is QosAtMostOnce -> 0
    is QosAtLeastOnce -> 1
    is QosExactlyOnce -> 2

Section: Variable-Length Encoding

  mqtt-encode-remaining-length : Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-encode-remaining-length (len) =
   mqtt-encode-vbi len []

  mqtt-encode-vbi : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-encode-vbi (value) (acc) =
   let byte = bit-and value 127
   in let next = bit-shru value 7
   in if next > 0 then mqtt-encode-vbi next (list-push acc (bit-or byte 128))
   else list-push acc byte

Section: String Encoding

  mqtt-encode-utf8 : Text -> List Integer
  mqtt-encode-utf8 (s) =
   let len = text-length s
   in let hi = bit-and (bit-shru len 8) 255
   in let lo = bit-and len 255
   in [hi, lo] & mqtt-text-bytes s 0 len []

  mqtt-text-bytes : Text, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-text-bytes (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else mqtt-text-bytes s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (to-unicode (char-code (char-at s i))))

Section: CONNECT Packet

  MqttConnectConfig = record {
   client-id : Text,
   keep-alive : Integer between 0 and 65535,
   clean-start : Boolean,
   username : Maybe Text,
   password : Maybe Text
  }

  default-mqtt-connect : MqttConnectConfig
  default-mqtt-connect = MqttConnectConfig {
   client-id = "codex-iot",
   keep-alive = 60,
   clean-start = True,
   username = None,
   password = None
  }

 THE CREDENTIALS WERE DEAD FIELDS. `MqttConnectConfig` has carried
 `username` and `password` since it was written, `mqtt-connect-flags`
 returned only the clean-start bit, and the payload was the client id and
 nothing else -- so a broker requiring authentication refused every
 connection this chapter could build, and the config said otherwise. They
 are emitted now, in the order MQTT v5 section 3.1.3 fixes: client id,
 then username if its flag is set, then password.

  mqtt-build-connect : MqttConnectConfig -> List Integer
  mqtt-build-connect (cfg) =
   let proto-name = mqtt-encode-utf8 "MQTT"
   in let proto-level = [5]
   in let flags = mqtt-connect-flags cfg
   in let keep-alive = [(bit-shru (cfg.keep-alive) 8), bit-and (cfg.keep-alive) 255]
   in let properties = [0]
   in let client-id = mqtt-encode-utf8 (cfg.client-id)
   in let user-part = when cfg.username is Just (u) -> mqtt-encode-utf8 u is None -> []
   in let pass-part = when cfg.password is Just (p) -> mqtt-encode-utf8 p is None -> []
   in let payload = client-id & user-part & pass-part
   in let var-header = proto-name & proto-level & [flags] & keep-alive & properties
   in let remaining = var-header & payload
   in let header = [bit-shl 1 4] & mqtt-encode-remaining-length (list-length remaining)
   in header & remaining

  mqtt-connect-flags : MqttConnectConfig -> Integer
  mqtt-connect-flags (cfg) =
   let clean = if cfg.clean-start then 2 else 0
   in let user = when cfg.username is Just (u) -> 128 is None -> 0
   in let pass = when cfg.password is Just (p) -> 64 is None -> 0
   in bit-or clean (bit-or user pass)

Section: PUBLISH Packet

 A PUBLISH AT QoS 1 OR 2 CARRIES A PACKET IDENTIFIER, immediately after
 the topic and before the properties (MQTT v5 section 3.3.2.2). This
 builder had no packet-id parameter at all, so at any QoS above zero it
 produced a packet whose property length was read as the high byte of an
 identifier and whose payload was off by two -- a malformed packet a
 broker closes the connection on. Nothing caught it because every caller
 in the tree publishes at QoS 0, where the field is absent and the old
 code was accidentally right.
 The identifier is ignored at QoS 0, where the protocol forbids it.

  mqtt-build-publish : Text, List Integer, MqttQos, Boolean, Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-build-publish (topic) (payload) (qos) (retain) (packet-id) =
   let flags = bit-or (bit-shl (mqtt-qos-value qos) 1) (if retain then 1 else 0)
   in let header-byte = bit-or (bit-shl 3 4) flags
   in let topic-bytes = mqtt-encode-utf8 topic
   in let pid = if mqtt-qos-value qos > 0 then [bit-and (bit-shru packet-id 8) 255, bit-and packet-id 255] else []
   in let properties = [0]
   in let var-header = topic-bytes & pid & properties
   in let remaining = var-header & payload
   in [header-byte] & mqtt-encode-remaining-length (list-length remaining) & remaining

Section: PUBACK Packet

 The acknowledgement a QoS 1 PUBLISH is answered with, and the one a
 subscriber must send for a QoS 1 delivery it receives. Neither direction
 could be expressed before: the packet type was in the enumeration and no
 builder produced it, so a client that subscribed at QoS 1 took delivery
 and never acknowledged, and the broker redelivered forever.

  mqtt-build-puback : Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-build-puback (packet-id) =
   [bit-shl 4 4, 2, bit-and (bit-shru packet-id 8) 255, bit-and packet-id 255]

Section: QoS 2 Packets

 The four-step exchange "exactly once" is made of, and the reason
 `QosExactlyOnce` was a constructor with no delivery guarantee behind it
 until 2026-07-23. A publisher sends PUBLISH and hears PUBREC; it answers
 PUBREL and hears PUBCOMP. A subscriber does the mirror. Two round trips
 buy what one cannot: the receiver may record the identifier before
 acknowledging, so a redelivery after a lost acknowledgement is
 recognised as the same message rather than delivered twice.

 PUBREL CARRIES FIXED-HEADER FLAGS 0010, not zero. It is the only one of
 the three that does, it is reserved and a broker MUST close the
 connection on any other value (section 3.6.1), and a builder that writes
 zero there produces a packet mosquitto disconnects on rather than
 answers -- which reads as a network fault.

  mqtt-build-pubrec : Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-build-pubrec (packet-id) =
   [bit-shl 5 4, 2, bit-and (bit-shru packet-id 8) 255, bit-and packet-id 255]

  mqtt-build-pubrel : Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-build-pubrel (packet-id) =
   [bit-or (bit-shl 6 4) 2, 2, bit-and (bit-shru packet-id 8) 255, bit-and packet-id 255]

  mqtt-build-pubcomp : Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-build-pubcomp (packet-id) =
   [bit-shl 7 4, 2, bit-and (bit-shru packet-id 8) 255, bit-and packet-id 255]

Section: SUBSCRIBE Packet

  mqtt-build-subscribe : Integer, Text, MqttQos -> List Integer
  mqtt-build-subscribe (packet-id) (topic-filter) (qos) =
   let header-byte = bit-or (bit-shl 8 4) 2
   in let pid = [(bit-shru packet-id 8), bit-and packet-id 255]
   in let properties = [0]
   in let topic-bytes = mqtt-encode-utf8 topic-filter
   in let sub-options = [mqtt-qos-value qos]
   in let remaining = pid & properties & topic-bytes & sub-options
   in [header-byte] & mqtt-encode-remaining-length (list-length remaining) & remaining

Section: PINGREQ Packet

  mqtt-build-pingreq : List Integer
  mqtt-build-pingreq = [bit-shl 12 4, 0]

Section: DISCONNECT Packet

  mqtt-build-disconnect : List Integer
  mqtt-build-disconnect = [bit-shl 14 4, 0]

Section: Decoding

 THIS CHAPTER COULD ONLY SPEAK. Every function above builds a packet and
 there was no parser at all, so CONNACK could not be read, a subscription
 could not be confirmed, and an inbound PUBLISH -- the entire point of
 subscribing -- was unreachable. A client built on this could send into a
 broker and never learn anything back, which is not a client.

 The decoders below are the reading half. They are deliberately tolerant
 of the v5 property block: every one of these packet bodies carries a
 variable-byte property length that a v3 parser would run straight past,
 and mosquitto populates it.

  MqttVbi = record {
    mv-value : Integer,
    mv-len : Integer,
    mv-ok : Boolean
  }

  mqtt-decode-vbi : List Integer, Integer -> MqttVbi
  mqtt-decode-vbi (bs) (at) = mqtt-decode-vbi-loop bs at 0 0 0

  mqtt-decode-vbi-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> MqttVbi
  mqtt-decode-vbi-loop (bs) (at) (i) (acc) (shift) =
   if i >= 4 then MqttVbi { mv-value = 0, mv-len = 0, mv-ok = False }
   else if at + i >= list-length bs then MqttVbi { mv-value = 0, mv-len = 0, mv-ok = False }
   else let b = list-at bs (at + i)
   in let acc2 = acc + bit-shl (bit-and b 127) shift
   in if bit-and b 128 == 0 then MqttVbi { mv-value = acc2, mv-len = i + 1, mv-ok = True }
   else mqtt-decode-vbi-loop bs at (i + 1) acc2 (shift + 7)

  mqtt-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-slice (bs) (from) (stop) = mqtt-slice-loop bs from stop []

  mqtt-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-slice-loop (bs) (i) (stop) (acc) =
   if i >= stop then acc
   else if i >= list-length bs then acc
   else mqtt-slice-loop bs (i + 1) stop (list-push acc (list-at bs i))

 A packet, split into its fixed header and everything after it. `mp-total`
 is what the caller must drop from its buffer -- TCP delivers a byte
 stream and a segment can carry half a packet or three, so a reader that
 does not know how much it consumed cannot find the next one.

  MqttPacket = record {
    mp-type : Integer,
    mp-flags : Integer,
    mp-total : Integer,
    mp-body : List Integer
  }

  mqtt-parse : List Integer -> Maybe MqttPacket
  mqtt-parse (bs) =
   if list-length bs < 2 then None
   else let b0 = list-at bs 0
   in let vbi = mqtt-decode-vbi bs 1
   in if vbi.mv-ok == False then None
   else let head = 1 + vbi.mv-len
   in let total = head + vbi.mv-value
   in if list-length bs < total then None
   else Just (MqttPacket {
     mp-type = bit-shru b0 4,
     mp-flags = bit-and b0 15,
     mp-total = total,
     mp-body = mqtt-slice bs head total
   })

 CONNACK: acknowledge flags, then the reason code. Zero is success and
 anything else is the broker refusing, with 135 "not authorized" and 133
 "client identifier not valid" the ones a caller most wants to tell apart.

  mqtt-connack-reason : MqttPacket -> Integer
  mqtt-connack-reason (p) =
   if list-length (p.mp-body) < 2 then 255 else list-at (p.mp-body) 1

 SUBACK: the packet identifier, then a property block, then one reason
 code per topic filter the SUBSCRIBE carried. A reason below 128 is the
 granted QoS; 128 and above is a refusal.

  mqtt-suback-id : MqttPacket -> Integer
  mqtt-suback-id (p) =
   if list-length (p.mp-body) < 2 then (-1)
   else list-at (p.mp-body) 0 * 256 + list-at (p.mp-body) 1

  mqtt-suback-reason : MqttPacket -> Integer
  mqtt-suback-reason (p) =
   let props = mqtt-decode-vbi (p.mp-body) 2
   in if props.mv-ok == False then 255
   else let at = 2 + props.mv-len + props.mv-value
   in if at >= list-length (p.mp-body) then 255
   else list-at (p.mp-body) at

  mqtt-puback-id : MqttPacket -> Integer
  mqtt-puback-id (p) =
   if list-length (p.mp-body) < 2 then (-1)
   else list-at (p.mp-body) 0 * 256 + list-at (p.mp-body) 1

 PUBACK, PUBREC, PUBREL and PUBCOMP all carry the same body -- a packet
 identifier and an optional reason -- so one reader serves all four. The
 neutral name exists because a caller reading a PUBCOMP through something
 called `puback-id` is a sentence that invites the wrong packet.

  mqtt-ack-id : MqttPacket -> Integer
  mqtt-ack-id (p) = mqtt-puback-id p

 An inbound PUBLISH. The QoS is in the FIXED HEADER flags, not the body,
 and it decides whether a packet identifier sits between the topic and
 the properties -- so the payload offset depends on a field three bytes
 earlier, which is the shape that makes a hand-written reader wrong.

  mqtt-publish-qos : MqttPacket -> Integer
  mqtt-publish-qos (p) = bit-and (bit-shru (p.mp-flags) 1) 3

  mqtt-publish-topic : MqttPacket -> List Integer
  mqtt-publish-topic (p) =
   if list-length (p.mp-body) < 2 then []
   else let n = list-at (p.mp-body) 0 * 256 + list-at (p.mp-body) 1
   in mqtt-slice (p.mp-body) 2 (2 + n)

  mqtt-publish-id : MqttPacket -> Integer
  mqtt-publish-id (p) =
   if mqtt-publish-qos p == 0 then (-1)
   else if list-length (p.mp-body) < 2 then (-1)
   else let n = list-at (p.mp-body) 0 * 256 + list-at (p.mp-body) 1
   in if 2 + n + 1 >= list-length (p.mp-body) then (-1)
   else list-at (p.mp-body) (2 + n) * 256 + list-at (p.mp-body) (2 + n + 1)

  mqtt-publish-payload : MqttPacket -> List Integer
  mqtt-publish-payload (p) =
   if list-length (p.mp-body) < 2 then []
   else let n = list-at (p.mp-body) 0 * 256 + list-at (p.mp-body) 1
   in let after-topic = 2 + n + (if mqtt-publish-qos p > 0 then 2 else 0)
   in let props = mqtt-decode-vbi (p.mp-body) after-topic
   in if props.mv-ok == False then []
   else mqtt-slice (p.mp-body) (after-topic + props.mv-len + props.mv-value) (list-length (p.mp-body))

